Proposed change: replace scrimRow's body (SleepAnimation.cpp, currently
~lines 402-423) with the scrimRow_branchlessWord shape derived and proven
in this directory (tools/animbench/kernels-scrimfix/scrimrow.cpp). This is
a diff, not an applied edit -- src/ is untouched by this task; apply with
`git apply` from the repo root if/when someone decides to ship it.

Rationale
---------
scrimRow gets ZERO Xtensa hardware zero-overhead LOOP instructions from GCC
despite being call-free (everything it calls -- scrimCell, scale565x2,
scale565 -- is always_inline): 94 instructions, 0 loops, confirmed via this
directory's own asm.sh against the real toolchain
(toolchain-xtensa-esp-elf, GCC 14.2.0, the same compiler
.pio/build/*/compile_commands.json confirms the firmware actually ships
with) at the real firmware's flags (-O2 -std=gnu++20 -mlongcalls
-fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections
-fno-jump-tables, cross-checked against platformio.ini's
[display_common]/[env:display] sections directly, since this checkout's
.pio/build/ has no display-env compile_commands.json to read from --
see this directory's asm.sh header for the full cross-check).

The obvious suspect is the per-cell `if (inv == SCRIM_INV_NONE) continue;`:
GCC's Xtensa backend only emits a zero-overhead LOOP for a fixed trip count
with no early exit, and this `continue` is data-dependent (keyed on
invRow[c], not on c). Removing JUST that branch -- calling scrimCell
unconditionally, safe because scale565's own doc comment establishes 32 is
an exact identity factor ("inv is 0..32, i.e. 32 keeps the pixel and 0
blacks it out") -- does NOT fix it: still 0 loops, and at 97 instructions
it is worse than the original 94 (confirmed here as scrimRow_branchlessCell
in scrimrow.cpp/scrimrow.S, independently, not assumed). Comparing the two
loop bodies directly finds the real blocker: scrimCell computes a cell's
TWO words in parallel (`q[0] = scale565x2(q[0], inv); q[1] =
scale565x2(q[1], inv);`), and that register pressure -- not the branch --
is what starves GCC's Xtensa doloop pass of the address register it needs
for the loop-count mechanism.

scrimRow_branchlessWord fixes the actual blocker: iterate one 32-bit WORD
(two pixels) per loop trip instead of one CELL (two words / four pixels),
keeping the same branchless SCRIM_INV_NONE substitution. This halves the
live temporaries per trip while doubling the trip count. The loop-counter
SHAPE also matters, confirmed the hard way: a first attempt walking an
absolute word index over the row (mathematically identical, verified
bit-exact, but NOT kept as the shipped shape) still got 0 loops at 61
instructions; rewriting to count up from zero with the pointer pre-offset
by the run's start (`uint32_t *const qBase = reinterpret_cast<uint32_t*>(dst
+ (c0 << SCRIM_SHIFT)); const int nWords = (c1 - c0) * 2; for (int wi = 0;
wi < nWords; wi++) ...`) is what actually recovers it: 64 instructions, 1
zero-overhead LOOP. This independently reproduces the exact figure a second
worker (tools/overlaybench/kernels/overlay_blend.cpp, working in parallel
on a related composite-pass optimization) reported for their own,
separately-derived equivalent function -- a real independent confirmation,
not an assumed match; the two derivations converged on the same loop shape
without sharing code.

Bit-exactness: proven over 20,416 randomized and directed test cases
(tools/animbench/kernels-scrimfix/prove_scrimfix.cpp) -- random invRow/runs/
dst content, nHalo==0, runs ending on an odd cell boundary, w not a multiple
of 4 or 8, all-SCRIM_INV_NONE rows, all-zero rows. All pass byte-for-byte
against the unmodified scrimRow.

Net effect at the call site (SleepAnimation.cpp ~3015-3024): none needed.
scrimRow is called unconditionally there when pieScrim is false
(`scrimRow(drow, invRow, halo, nHalo, w);`) -- this patch only changes
scrimRow's own body, so the call site's text is untouched and every caller
picks up the fix automatically.

Not addressed by this patch: the composite/blend pass (blendRow) is a
separate optimization target (see the FINDINGS-OVERLAY.md/kernels-blend
work happening in parallel) and is untouched here.

--- a/src/display/ui/default/SleepAnimation.cpp
+++ b/src/display/ui/default/SleepAnimation.cpp
@@ -399,6 +399,26 @@
 // The dim is keyed on the cell rather than on a pixel's own coverage on
 // purpose: the pixels that decide legibility are the transparent ones between
 // strokes and inside glyph counters, which the blend never touches at all.
+//
+// One 32-bit WORD (two pixels) per loop trip, not one cell (four pixels,
+// scrimCell's own unit) -- and counted up from zero, not walked over an
+// absolute word index -- is what actually gets this loop a hardware
+// zero-overhead LOOP. The per-cell `if (inv == SCRIM_INV_NONE) continue;`
+// this replaced looked like the obvious blocker (GCC's Xtensa backend needs
+// a fixed trip count for LOOP, and a data-dependent `continue` breaks that),
+// but removing just the branch does not fix it: 0 loops, 97 instructions,
+// worse than this function's original 94, because scrimCell's two words
+// computed back-to-back are what starve the loop-count register, not the
+// branch. Restructuring to one word per trip, counted from zero so the trip
+// count is its own SSA value at loop entry rather than a difference GCC has
+// to re-derive from a loop-carried index, recovers it: 64 instructions, 1
+// zero-overhead LOOP. Both the branch removal alone and this word-restructure
+// are bit-exact substitutions -- see the identity argument below and
+// tools/animbench/kernels-scrimfix/scrimrow.cpp for the full derivation
+// (including the rejected absolute-index shape, which is bit-exact and
+// halves the per-trip body the same way but still doesn't recover the
+// loop), the randomized bit-exactness proof, and the real-toolchain asm
+// evidence for all three shapes (tools/animbench/kernels-scrimfix/asm.sh).
 __attribute__((noinline)) static void scrimRow(uint16_t *__restrict dst, const uint8_t *__restrict invRow,
                                                const uint32_t *__restrict runs, int nRuns, int w) {
     for (int i = 0; i < nRuns; i++) {
@@ -408,16 +428,20 @@
         if ((c1 << SCRIM_SHIFT) > w) {
             c1 = w >> SCRIM_SHIFT;
         }
-        for (int c = c0; c < c1; c++) {
-            // Already the 1/32 factor, not the coverage it came from: the
-            // strength multiply, the clamp and the rounding are the same for
-            // every frame the overlay lives through, so buildScrim does them
-            // once per publish instead of 24,000 times per frame.
-            const uint32_t inv = invRow[c];
-            if (inv == SCRIM_INV_NONE) {
-                continue; // a cell a gap merge swallowed
-            }
-            scrimCell(dst, inv, c);
+        // scrimCell maps cell c to words 2c and 2c+1 of the row (dst is
+        // 4-byte aligned to start and a scrim cell starts on an even pixel,
+        // see the comment on scale565x2 above), so this cast agrees with
+        // scrimCell's own reinterpret_cast<uint32_t*>(dst + (c<<2)) on where
+        // a cell's two words live.
+        uint32_t *const qBase = reinterpret_cast<uint32_t *>(dst + (c0 << SCRIM_SHIFT));
+        const int nWords = (c1 - c0) * 2;
+        for (int wi = 0; wi < nWords; wi++) {
+            // No skip on SCRIM_INV_NONE: scale565(c, 32) == c exactly (see
+            // scale565's own doc comment above -- "32 keeps the pixel"), so
+            // scaling a cell a gap merge already left at full strength is a
+            // wasted multiply, not a wrong pixel.
+            const uint32_t inv = invRow[c0 + (wi >> 1)];
+            qBase[wi] = scale565x2(qBase[wi], inv);
         }
     }
 }
